,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,0,\\,-4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u & 0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 2v & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & -4v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,-4{cos}^{2}⁡u,\\,2v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388082 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388089 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387984 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387991 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124482 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387998 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386646 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386681 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386695 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386702 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124560 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388388 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124583 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386709 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386201 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388187 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186124613 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386208 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386088 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388292 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388299 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388306 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388285 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388264 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129199 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388271 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129200 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388278 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386377 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387055 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387062 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186125978 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186129418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386107 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129418 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x = \\tan y$ that lies between $y = 0$ and $y = \\frac{π}{4}$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186129418 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129418 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186125990 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186129420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386384 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129420 1071 "![](images/dc/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x = 2y - y^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186129420 1071 "![](images/dc/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186129420 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387028 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387035 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387560 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387567 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386396 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386403 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387476 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387483 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186133898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387490 131