f the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167055 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163474 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186167071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386276 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167071 1071 "The second-order differential equation $y^{″} - 2y^{′} + y = 0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where\n\n$x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}, y(t) = x_{1}(t), y^{′}(t) = x_{2}(t)$.\nFind the matrix $A$." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186167071 1071 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167071 1074 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163480 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186167072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386282 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167072 1071 "The second-order differential equation\n\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 4y = 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$ and $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186167072 1071 "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186167072 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{′}+4y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={x}_{2}+4{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386951 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172041 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386958 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172043 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386944 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172046 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386965 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172046 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386972 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172066 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386472 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186172066 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386479 13194139533462 17