^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1+{y}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2y.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387698 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175990 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386451 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175990 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386458 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175993 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387691 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175993 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387705 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175993 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387712 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386410 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386417 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175995 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386424 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175996 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386431 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186175998 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387719 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386563 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386570 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386549 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386556 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386363 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386370 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386639 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386612 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386619 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386626 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173512 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386101 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176214 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y = \\sin x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = \\frac{π}{2}$. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176214 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176214 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173596 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386270 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176228 1071 "The third-order differential equation\n$y^{‴} - \\sqrt{t}y^{″} - y = t^{2}$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176228 1071 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}-\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}-y={t}^{2}\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176228 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},$ ${x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{y}^{″}+y+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{x}_{1}+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t)={x}_{1}+\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}+{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173614 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386301 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176231 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176231 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176231 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173662 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386390 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176239 1071 "![](images/12/12d30907e2fb6cdd031134a667c7de1324fec66025c0f2029b18f4521e94da83.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x = 3 + 2y - y^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176239 1071 "![](images/12/12d30907e2fb6cdd031134a667c7de1324fec66025c0f2029b18f4521e94da83.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=3+2y-{y}^{2}$ that lies to the right of the $y$-axis between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176239 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y-3 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-1$ to $y=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3+2y-{y}^{2})=2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}-8y+4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3+2y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173722 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386438 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176249 1071 "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y = x$, $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176249 1071 "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176249 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173950 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386633 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176287 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186176287 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186176287 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-5I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387241 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387248 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387255 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387539 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387546 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387553 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387601 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387608 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179695 103