true] [17592186179744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387635 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387469 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387511 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387525 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387532 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177339 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387049 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179950 1071 "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) \\ge 0$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179950 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179950 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/59/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/43/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/18/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177429 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387181 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179965 1071 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = x + y^{2}$, traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179965 1071 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179965 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177471 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387187 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179972 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - y$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179972 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179972 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =2\\cdot (-2)\\,i-j=-4\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =2\\cdot (0)\\,i-j=-\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\cdot 2\\,i-j=4\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177513 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387304 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179979 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{x^{3} - 2y^{2}}{4}$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179979 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179979 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177615 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387457 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179996 1071 "![](images/a4/a4a1d43bc68a44db24d22767630573ebd9aafb646196e212640c24f2650e7b17.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1-u}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179996 1071 "![](images/a4/a4a1d43bc68a44db24d22767630573ebd9aafb646196e212640c24f2650e7b17.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1 - u}$.\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179996 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/0d/0d736c2e3fa2c4ba1872b045b8bdcf4def98a24337683e368bec337b810b6acb.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-1),(1,0),$ and $(-2,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1aed421f9d6458fb8b72a44c83703b3f1c0475cee9ef9ec2f0a4cfcaad74d7fc.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=3>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation." 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177621 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387463 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179997 1071 "![](images/f5/f573a36be6a90a61b0d2b711b2eab5f630464bc1ecfad75cdccd9cf9cb62aa77.png)\n\nConsider the segment $PQ^(―)$ above, and the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = v - 2u - 3, y = u + 2v - 1$.\n\nThe image of $PQ^(―)$ under the action of $T$ is the segment $P^{′}Q^{′}^(―)$ in the $xy$-plane. Which of the following diagrams shows $P^{′}Q^{′}^(―)$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186179997 1071 "![](images/f5/f573a36be6a90a61b0d2b711b2eab5f630464bc1ecfad75cdccd9cf9cb62aa77.png)\n\nConsider the segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, and the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-2u-3,\\,y=u+2v-1.\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ under the action of $T$ is the segment $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}$ in the $xy$-plane. Which of the following diagrams shows $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186179997 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-2u-3 \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u+v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,0)$ and $(-8,-1).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/bc/bce5c5155a7e8f1f257b85712627d44dfcc467911a6638016bc3df5892054fef.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177735 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186180016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387574 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180016 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = e^{x}i + \\ln yj$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3, 2)$ to the point $B(1, 5)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = (3 - 2t)i + (2 + 3t)j$.*" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186180016 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3,2)$ to the point $B(1,5)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j.$*" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180016 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i+2\\,j$ and $b=i+5\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t[(i+5\\,j)-(3\\,i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i+2\\,j)+t(-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(3-2t)\\,i+(2+3t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3-2t,\\,y=2+3t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\cdot (-2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177783 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186180024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387629 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180024 1071 "Let $F(x, y, z) = y^{2}i - x^{2}j + 5k$ and let $C$ be a path in $R^{3}$. Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 false] [17592186180024 1071 "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186180024 1074 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386500 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388124 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388131 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387739 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387746 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387753 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186198362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387352 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186198479 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388103 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186198479 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388110 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186233202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386730 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385979 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1069 "q-101022" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1071 "Let $A(2,7,0)$ and $B(2,5,4).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{AB}.$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1072 #uuid "ed7c86d6-fb53-53c2-abe3-3eb800a8608d" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385978 1074 "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2i+7j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=2i+5j+4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =2i+5j+4k-(2i+7j) \\\\  & =-2j+4k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot ⟨0,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385980 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385981 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385982 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385983 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385984 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385982 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385979 1080 #uuid "1cb9bc3b-ff9a-5d0a-9466-e15a7ff5d1cc" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385980 1081 "⟨0,\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385980 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385980 1084 #uuid "3b601022-bed0-54ac-a0ea-07f24a77faf4" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385981 1081 "⟨0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385981 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385981 1084 #uuid "546cddcd-703a-51e2-ae7c-710a0e005f8c" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385982 1081 "⟨0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186385982 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17