86199 1084 #uuid "fcc87696-0f7f-5357-9620-dd2e02cbb9a9" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386200 1081 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386200 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386200 1084 #uuid "7bf9de78-7ed1-584c-bc4f-51bca014dd3f" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386202 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1069 "q-106082" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨3,3⟩,$ respectively." 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1072 #uuid "20d6d567-2d4c-5711-a61a-965584379b0f" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386201 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨3,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,3)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,3⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ \\sqrt{25-{3}^{2}}-\\sqrt{25-{0}^{2}} & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩ \\\\ 4-5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}},0⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{\\sqrt{25-{c}_{1}^{2}}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{\\sqrt{25-{c}_{1}^{2}}},0⟩\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-\\frac{3{c}_{1}}{\\sqrt{25-{c}_{1}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{25-{c}_{1}^{2}} & =3{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve this radical equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25-{c}_{1}^{2}} & =3{c}_{1} \\\\ 25-{c}_{1}^{2} & =9{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{1}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the radical equation, we notice that ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}$ does not satisfy the equation. Therefore, the only solution is ${c}_{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{3-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}={c}_{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}).$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386203 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386204 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386205 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386206 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386207 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1079 #edb/ref 17592186386207 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386202 1080 #uuid