592186386208 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨0,4,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,4,0)-f(-1,0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,4,0⟩-⟨-1,0,3⟩) \\\\ -16-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,4,-3⟩ \\\\ -17 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,4,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-2y,0⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-17 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,4,-3⟩ \\\\ -17 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2},0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,4,-3⟩ \\\\ -17 & =2{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-3}{(-3)} \\\\ x+1 & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{3-z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t-1,\\,y=4t,\\,z=3-3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t-1,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=3-3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2}=-17,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-1)-8(4t) & =-17 \\\\ 2t-2-32t & =-17 \\\\ -30t & =-15 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}-1=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =3-3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533462 17