t $3$ hours, we stop the line at $t=3.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $1\\cdot 3=3\\,\\text{km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(3,3).$\n\n![](images/7a/7a9fd32e5705860064e597fcf9308fee457063e060d3adede260f109ca488201.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $3$ hours, until $t=3+3=6.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7e47150bf1c205c243c6fa1d0f0e24b7a69f27a8461791d495eb7487cf4f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=3,$ the particle has an acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.5.$ The third line segment stops after $3$ hours. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(6,3)$ and has a slope of $0.5,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5(t-6) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5t-3 \\\\ v & =0.5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=6+3=9$ hours, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.5t \\\\ v & =0.5(9) \\\\ v & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(9,4.5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](images/a3/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png)" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386290 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386291 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386292 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386293 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386294 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386289 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186