592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386377 1071 "![](images/92/924444fa2da1709d788d3b3522e86743b46ee1c5a25999bb77731449e621a07d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=1-(x+1)^{2}$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386377 1072 #uuid "caf996b3-98c2-50cc-98cb-eb0ffa63955b" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386377 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-2,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-(x+1)^{2})=-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-(x+1)^{2})\\sqrt{1+(-2x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386379 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186386378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386380 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [