6045585 true] [17592186387167 1071 "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),2{t}^{4}+3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387167 1072 #uuid "84c7731a-fdb1-5923-b13d-b64da6ea7881" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387167 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}},24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1+1)^{2}},24({1}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387169 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387170 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387171 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387172 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387173 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387168 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592