true] [17592186387193 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨10,-1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387193 1072 #uuid "fb4a6e90-038b-5f2c-a298-f65f1f8dbd3f" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387193 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨10,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨3({3}^{2})-3+{C}_{1},\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-1⟩ & =⟨24+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-14$ and ${C}_{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t-14,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨3({1}^{2})-1-14,\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-1-7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387195 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387196 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387197 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387198 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387199 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387194 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17