n ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/5c/5c38b3ccfc99ca173324e4caf4585e07991162c75741b20b5d288ce73ad0ff98.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,1),(0,3),$ and $(-1,3).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcadc0b9909159ebf99280e8d47d52891d2aa8653d9bc89bb2a3bb8363d4fb1.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=1>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation." 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387512 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387513 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387512 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387514 13194139533462 17592186045