frac{2}{3}⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387687 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387687 1084 #uuid "8d0b4537-94ea-5372-b0ce-d2a9ce2af92a" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387688 1081 "⟨2,3,1⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387688 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387688 1084 #uuid "e3f103dd-6751-5e92-9040-e3091640d1c1" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387689 1081 "⟨4,6,2⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387689 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387689 1084 #uuid "98bb1e46-4483-567a-8928-ad8ffc8518da" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387690 1081 "⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387690 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387690 1084 #uuid "0287d710-445d-5893-9720-79a914820f10" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387692 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1069 "q-126514" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1071 "![](images/c1/c1f8bb7ed82d345f8397ea9b01854f05f3f668acdcebf5d75ebbc5cb8de04fa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1072 #uuid "21730a28-6e7f-5db6-af91-78ecf92baa4a" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387691 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/9e/9eceb8ab173995a8325fb1a8452baaa7f1071e569391e9a49b69c047d76f3b52.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{y+1})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387693 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387694 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387695 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387696 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387697 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1077 "selection" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1079 #edb/ref 17592186387694 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [17592186387692 1080 #uuid "6bee84b5-4364-5ef7-b230-57a279a56f9a" 13194139533462 17592186045585 true] [175921863